# Milestone 4: Intelligent Test Case Generation Engine

## Project Context

I am developing an Autonomous Software Testing Agent called Autonomous QA Studio.

The existing application contains:

- Angular frontend.
- Python FastAPI backend.
- OpenAI integration.
- Text-based test case generation.
- Multi-source document ingestion.
- PDF, DOCX, XLSX, CSV, PPTX and image extraction.
- Embedded screenshot extraction.
- AI-based requirement understanding.
- Unified Requirement Specification (URS).
- Requirement consolidation and conflict detection.
- Human review and approval of requirements.

Now I want to implement an intelligent test case generation engine.

The engine must take an approved URS and generate comprehensive, structured and traceable test cases.

## 1. Inspect the Existing Project

Before implementation:

1. Inspect the existing backend and frontend.
2. Understand the current URS schema.
3. Inspect how approved specifications are stored.
4. Inspect the existing text-based test generation implementation.
5. Understand existing OpenAI integration.
6. Inspect current test case schemas and API endpoints.
7. Identify reusable components.

Important:
- Preserve existing functionality.
- Reuse the existing AI integration.
- Do not create duplicate test generation services.
- Do not break the existing text-based generation endpoint.
- Do not regenerate requirements unnecessarily.
- Use approved URS records as the authoritative source.

## 2. Test Planning Engine

Before generating individual test cases, create a Test Planning Engine.

Its responsibility is to analyze approved requirements and determine:

- What functionality needs testing.
- What business rules need validation.
- What user workflows need testing.
- What UI components need testing.
- What boundary conditions exist.
- What negative scenarios are relevant.
- What security scenarios are appropriate.
- Which requirements depend on other requirements.

The planning engine should create a structured test plan.

Example:

{
  "test_plan_id": "PLAN_001",
  "specification_id": "URS_001",
  "modules": [
    {
      "module_name": "User Registration",
      "requirement_ids": ["REQ_001", "REQ_002"],
      "test_categories": [
        "positive",
        "negative",
        "boundary",
        "validation"
      ]
    }
  ]
}

Avoid generating irrelevant test categories for every requirement.

## 3. Test Case Generation

Generate test cases based on approved requirements.

Support the following categories:

### Functional Testing
- Positive scenarios.
- Negative scenarios.
- Business workflow validation.
- Functional behavior.

### Validation Testing
- Mandatory field validation.
- Incorrect input formats.
- Invalid data.
- Missing values.
- Field constraints.

### Boundary Testing
- Minimum values.
- Maximum values.
- Values just below limits.
- Values just above limits.
- Empty and null values where relevant.

### Security Testing
- Input injection scenarios.
- Authentication checks.
- Authorization checks.
- Session-related checks.
- Sensitive data exposure checks.

Only generate security scenarios relevant to the available requirements and application context. Mark scenarios requiring security-specific specifications or authorization as assumptions.

### UI Interaction Testing
- Button interactions.
- Form submission.
- Navigation.
- Input field behavior.
- Dropdown selection.
- Checkbox and radio button behavior.
- Error message visibility.
- Screen transitions.

### Visual Testing
Generate visual validation scenarios from approved UI mockups and screen specifications.

Examples:
- Verify expected UI components are present.
- Verify field alignment.
- Verify component positioning.
- Verify colors and typography against the reference.
- Verify spacing and layout.
- Verify responsive layout when appropriate reference designs are available.

Keep visual expectations linked to the original mockup or screenshot.

### State Transition Testing
- Valid state transitions.
- Invalid transitions.
- Workflow completion.
- Unauthenticated versus authenticated states.
- Pending, approved, rejected or completed states where relevant.

### Edge Case Testing
Identify meaningful unusual conditions based on requirements.

Do not create arbitrary edge cases without a reasonable basis.

## 4. Test Case Structure

Every test case must follow a consistent Pydantic schema.

Suggested structure:

{
  "test_case_id": "TC_001",
  "title": "Verify registration with valid credentials",
  "description": "Verify that a new user can register successfully.",
  "requirement_ids": ["REQ_001"],
  "category": "positive",
  "test_type": "functional",
  "priority": "high",
  "preconditions": [
    "Registration page is accessible",
    "Email is not already registered"
  ],
  "test_data": {
    "email": "test@example.com",
    "password": "ValidPassword123"
  },
  "steps": [
    {
      "step_number": 1,
      "action": "Enter a valid email address",
      "expected_result": "Email is accepted"
    },
    {
      "step_number": 2,
      "action": "Enter a valid password",
      "expected_result": "Password is accepted"
    },
    {
      "step_number": 3,
      "action": "Click Register",
      "expected_result": "Registration is submitted successfully"
    }
  ],
  "expected_results": [
    "Registration succeeds",
    "Verification email is triggered"
  ],
  "automation_feasibility": "high",
  "automation_notes": [],
  "assumptions": [],
  "confidence": 0.95,
  "source_references": [
    {
      "requirement_id": "REQ_001"
    }
  ]
}

Adapt this schema to the existing architecture.

Use stable, unique test case IDs.

Ensure test steps are detailed enough for a future browser automation engine to interpret.

## 5. UI Mockup-Based Test Generation

Use the UI component specifications and original image references stored in the approved URS.

For each screen:

- Identify relevant interactive components.
- Generate potential interaction scenarios.
- Generate validation scenarios when supported by requirements.
- Generate navigation scenarios when the destination is known.
- Generate visual comparison scenarios.
- Link each test case to its screen ID and source image ID.

Important:

A static screenshot does not establish the actual behavior of a button, field or link.

If expected behavior is not specified:
- Mark the scenario as inferred.
- Include the assumption.
- Do not represent inferred behavior as a confirmed requirement.

For visual tests, preserve the reference image ID so the future Playwright engine can retrieve the original image.

## 6. Requirement Traceability

Every generated test case must reference one or more approved requirement IDs.

Implement traceability in both directions:

Requirement -> Generated Test Cases

Test Case -> Source Requirements

Generate a coverage summary:

{
  "total_requirements": 20,
  "covered_requirements": 17,
  "uncovered_requirements": 3,
  "coverage_percentage": 85,
  "uncovered_requirement_ids": [
    "REQ_014",
    "REQ_017",
    "REQ_020"
  ]
}

Calculate coverage from actual mappings, not from LLM-generated percentages.

Identify:
- Requirements with no test cases.
- Requirements with insufficient scenario coverage.
- Test cases without valid requirement references.
- Requirements covered only by inferred test cases.

Do not claim 100% coverage simply because every requirement has one test case.

## 7. Test Case Quality Validation Engine

After generation, validate the test cases.

Check for:

- Duplicate test cases.
- Missing test steps.
- Missing expected results.
- Invalid requirement references.
- Inconsistent priority.
- Invalid categories.
- Missing preconditions where needed.
- Ambiguous test actions.
- Unsupported assumptions.
- Contradictory expected results.

Implement deterministic validation wherever possible.

The LLM may assist in semantic duplicate detection, but use application logic for schema and reference validation.

Invalid test cases must not silently become approved test cases.

## 8. Test Suite Organization

Group generated test cases by:

- Project.
- Specification version.
- Module.
- Requirement.
- Test category.
- Priority.

Allow multiple test suites for a project.

Support regeneration when requirements change without unnecessarily deleting previously approved test cases.

Preserve relationships between test suites and specification versions.

## 9. Human Review

Before execution, allow users to review generated test cases.

Users should be able to:

- View individual test cases.
- Edit titles and descriptions.
- Edit test steps.
- Edit expected results.
- Change priority.
- Add test data.
- Delete irrelevant test cases.
- Approve test cases.
- Reject test cases.

Track whether a test case is:
- Generated.
- Needs review.
- Approved.
- Rejected.
- Modified by user.

Do not automatically approve all generated cases.

## 10. Backend APIs

Follow existing FastAPI conventions.

Suggested APIs:

POST /api/test-suites/generate

Input:
- Approved specification ID.
- Optional selected requirement IDs.
- Optional generation settings.

Behavior:
- Retrieve approved URS.
- Generate test plan.
- Generate test cases.
- Validate generated test cases.
- Calculate requirement coverage.
- Save the test suite.
- Return structured results.

GET /api/test-suites/{suite_id}

Retrieve the complete test suite.

GET /api/test-suites/{suite_id}/coverage

Retrieve requirement coverage information.

PATCH /api/test-cases/{test_case_id}

Update an individual test case.

POST /api/test-cases/{test_case_id}/approve

Approve a test case.

POST /api/test-cases/{test_case_id}/reject

Reject a test case.

Adapt endpoint naming to the existing architecture.

